WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:03.280
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here!

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:07.460
Marvel's latest movie, Doctor Strange, takes
us into a world of dazzling new dimensions

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:11.920
and amazing magical powers that look truly
extraordinary up on the big screen.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.219
Over the course of the film, director Scott
Derrickson leads us creatively through a wealth

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:21.249
of exceptionally striking, wildly out-there
images of fantastical events, and as he does

00:00:21.249 --> 00:00:25.160
so he sometimes draws in intriguing ways on
his background as a writer and director of

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:27.590
horror movies like the consistently creepy
Sinister.

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:32.599
In fact, for both the characters and the audience,
the movie's visuals and action scenes frequently

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:36.480
blur the lines between waking nightmare, dream,
and hallucination.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.300
The movie manages to literally up-end action
genre tropes like on-foot chases and ticking

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:46.460
clock scenarios, and gives them a nicely inventive
spin to create totally trippy, wacked-out

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:47.460
sequences.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:51.560
Forget your boring old laws of physics; in
Doctor Strange practically anything goes!

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:56.380
Streets, buildings, walls, ceilings, floors,
and more bend forwards, backwards, and in

00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:00.660
on one another, and turn upside down, and
twist around in constant motion.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:05.390
Yes, there are Inception and Matrix-style
moments, but there's much more than that.

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:11.679
Doctor Strange is a wondrous whirl of mind-bending
movement, coupled with a kaleidoscope of fractured

00:01:11.679 --> 00:01:15.909
shapes and colours that sends us hurtling
through different dimensions.

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:20.689
There are even times in this Steve Ditko-inspired,
psychedelic world when you actually feel like

00:01:20.689 --> 00:01:22.810
you're looking at a moving comic book page.

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:27.130
The visuals are so spectacular, and there's
so much going on in them at the same time,

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:29.109
that it's almost hard to know where to look.

00:01:29.109 --> 00:01:33.289
In fact, it's such a feast for your eyes that
it's a movie worth seeing more than once on

00:01:33.289 --> 00:01:34.289
the big screen.

00:01:34.289 --> 00:01:38.920
And just like Jon Favreau's live-action Jungle
Book, Doctor Strange is a movie that really

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:41.359
does reap the rewards of 3D.

00:01:41.359 --> 00:01:46.240
Scott Derrickson and his super-skilled visual
effects teams have clearly gone all-out to

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:49.130
make the very most of that technology – and
it shows.

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:52.889
Also, on the visuals side, I've got to give
credit to production designer Charles Wood,

00:01:52.889 --> 00:01:57.310
and the movie's art direction and set decoration
teams for creating beautifully detailed sets

00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:00.679
including Hong Kong streets, New York homes,
and Kamar Taj.

00:02:00.679 --> 00:02:02.020
Then, of course, there's the movie's actors.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:06.670
And what a captivating cast Marvel's managed
to assemble for Doctor Strange.

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:10.920
Obviously, we already knew that Benedict Cumberbatch
could play a charming, arrogant genius in

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:15.159
his sleep – we are talking, after all, about
the man who brought Sherlock Holmes to 21st-century

00:02:15.159 --> 00:02:17.610
life to such well-deserved critical acclaim.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:22.310
And he does an equally top-notch job as the
self-important neurosurgeon Stephen Strange.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:27.360
Yes, Doctor Strange is a role that fits Cumberbatch
like a glove, just as was the case with Iron

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:28.360
Man and Robert Downey Junior.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:33.069
You'll remember there was a good deal of controversy
over the casting of Tilda Swinton in the role

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:36.170
of the Ancient One, given the character's
an Asian man in the comics.

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:39.980
Focusing solely on her performance in the
film though, Swinton manages to be ethereal,

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:45.400
bad-ass, and sharply witty, all at once, even
when she's having to explain so much of the

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:46.400
movie's mythology.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.870
Chiwetel Ejiofor brings poise and a certain
class to the character of Mordo, who's studied

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:55.140
under the Ancient One, and whose function
in the film is too often exposition.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:59.050
By tweaking the comic-book role of Christine
Palmer, played by Rachel McAdams, the movie

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:02.719
quietly acknowledges that if you're a woman
working in a hospital, you may actually be

00:03:02.719 --> 00:03:04.090
a doctor, not a nurse.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:07.931
However, the character of Palmer as it's written
isn't particularly scintillating, and it's

00:03:07.931 --> 00:03:12.360
a testament to the talents of McAdams that
she makes the part work, giving Palmer a level

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:13.680
of emotional depth.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:17.769
As for Benedict Wong's character Wong, he
gets a welcome makeover from the comics.

00:03:17.769 --> 00:03:22.640
So instead of being Doctor Strange's manservant,
he's now a steely, rule-enforcing librarian

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:23.700
at Kamar-Taj.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:28.260
The scenes the two Benedicts share together
are a treat, and I'd love to see more of them.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:31.950
Just like the rest of the cast, Mads Mikkelsen
is an incredible actor, but as the movie's

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:36.610
baddie Kaecilius, the script doesn't give
him enough opportunity or on-screen time to

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:37.610
connect with us.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:41.510
Sure, he gets to be part of some cool, visually
stunning scenes, but in terms of dialogue

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:43.430
and motivation, it's pretty thin stuff.

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:47.170
Having said that, when Mikkelsen does appear
on screen, it's still always a pleasure to

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:48.170
see him.

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:51.680
I just hope his role in Rogue One is more
worthy of his talents.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.360
This being a Marvel superhero movie, you can
expect a good deal of comedy.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:57.739
As is often the case with comedy, some of
it works, while some doesn't land.

00:03:57.739 --> 00:04:01.450
For me, the better comic moments come from
the interaction between the two Benedicts,

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:06.460
Tilda Swinton's at-times-almost-gleefully-winking
Ancient One, and Doctor Strange's Cloak of

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:11.280
Levitation, which has a playful air of Aladdin's
magic carpet meets baby Groot about it; though,

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.409
let's face it, it's hard for anything to outdo
a talking, dancing tree.

00:04:15.409 --> 00:04:19.239
As you may have gathered from everything I've
said so far, sadly, Doctor Strange's script

00:04:19.239 --> 00:04:24.240
doesn't live up to the promise of its spellbinding
visual design and its incredible cast.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.510
We're fourteen films into the Marvel Cinematic
Universe now, and, I guess, unsurprisingly

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:32.270
in some ways, Doctor Strange's story feels
more than a tad familiar.

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:36.729
In fact, it's almost like we've come full
circle, back to 2008's Iron Man movie as this

00:04:36.729 --> 00:04:40.550
latest film basically follows Tony Stark's
character arc.

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:44.289
Of course, there's always a certain amount
of "been there done that" in any superhero

00:04:44.289 --> 00:04:48.590
origin story, and perhaps there was a sense
among the scriptwriters and producers that

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:53.330
the outrageously off-kilter reality of this
movie's world was more than enough to make

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:54.330
it different.

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:59.500
Or maybe, they thought that that stunningly
surreal world and its bizarre mythology was

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:04.140
already more than enough for the audience
to cope with, without adding further deviations

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:06.620
from the already well-worn origins-story route.

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:09.180
And, to an extent, they're right on both counts.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:14.330
After all, the film's dizzying visuals do
make it stand out from Marvel's other movies,

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:18.930
and there's so much head-spinning craziness
in Doctor Strange's story that it would be

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:23.340
rather a tall order to do what Civil War did
so well for Black Panther and, the admittedly

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:27.710
already well-known Spider-Man, and just plonk
Strange in the middle of Avengers: Infinity

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:31.580
War without giving him a proper, feature-length
introduction like this one.

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:35.940
However, with a stronger, more daring script
from Derrickson and C Robert Cargill, who

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:40.750
worked together on Sinister, and their co-writer
Jon Spaihts, Doctor Strange could have been

00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:43.560
one of Marvel's very best movies ever.

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:48.210
Still, this Doctor Strange movie does show
much promise for future outings of the Master

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:53.240
of the Mystic Arts, and if Marvel keeps up
this level of eye-popping imagery and finesses

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:56.699
the scripts, we're in for one hell of a trippy
ride in later instalments!

00:05:56.699 --> 00:06:01.740
By the way, Doctor Strange is such a stand-alone
story that you could even enjoy it without

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:04.930
ever having seen a single Marvel movie or
TV show.

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:09.121
That said, there are Easter Eggs and references
in there for fans of Marvel's wider work on

00:06:09.121 --> 00:06:13.340
the big screen and in the comic books, though
I've got to say, there've been so many brilliant

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:17.370
Stan Lee cameos that this one pales somewhat
in comparison.

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:22.460
To sum up: Doctor Strange is a dizzying, dazzling
visual delight with stellar work from the

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:26.979
design, art, costume, and effects departments
under Scott Derrickson's capable direction.

00:06:26.979 --> 00:06:31.389
And the wonderfully accomplished cast squeeze
the very most out of the script's predictable

00:06:31.389 --> 00:06:33.419
plotting and reams of exposition.

00:06:33.419 --> 00:06:36.350
Doctor Strange gets 3 and a half out of 5
Flicks.

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:39.830
Now, what did you think of Doctor Strange,
if you've seen it already, or what are you

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:42.020
most looking forward to about the film?

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:44.390
And how do you feel it compares to Marvel's
other movies?

00:06:44.390 --> 00:06:46.349
